A||||25||GTDude||What 3.1 has most horsepower and fit in place of the 2.8 easily !||||1||
Z||000000||GTDude||12-20-2001||11:42 AM||||As many of you have read......i blew the engine up in my 86 GT last night....the saying true....they run the best before they blow.....I'd like to know if anyone knows horsepower ratings of the 3.1's and which ones will easily adapt to my GT.  I may go that direction since im carless at the moment, but will have another that I lent a friend back in a week or so.  ANY help will be very much appreciated.  Thanks guyz.<P>Phil Pettus<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||12-20-2001||11:51 AM||||3.1 out of a Grand Prix is 150hp before 1994 and 165 now.<BR>3.4 is about 180HP with the Fiero intake system.<BR>The difference's in the HP is the Throttle body size. A 3.1 @ 150 has a 48mm TB the 165hp has a 52mm Tb (same size as a Fiero TB)<BR>The 3.4 has 160HP with a 48mm TB and with a 52mm gets about 180hp.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Steve Normington||12-20-2001||12:40 PM||||Make sure that you get a 3.1 or a 3.4, not a 3100 or 3400. 3100 or 3400's use aluminum heads are are not a direct replacement for the 2.8. Most of the newer engines out there are going to be the 3100 or 3400. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Voytek||12-20-2001||12:51 PM||||Let me put in a comment before you get 20 replies urging you to go to a 3.4.<P>A 60 degree 3.1 (I believe 1992 or 1993 and older) will fit exactly like your 2.8.  If I recall, they were rated at 160 HP.  The aluminum head engines were a bit more powerful, I believe.  The aluminum head engine will most likely have a coil pack instead of a distributor.  It is STILL a direct fit but it means that you will have to use the 3.1 intake (can't swap the red Fiero intake), relocate the coil pack (otherwise it will be on the firewall side and probably interfere with the cooling line - it did on my '87 GT) and last, but not least you will need to swap the computer.<P>Some will tell you that the exhaust manifolds won't fit.  YES THEY DO.  It is an exact swap.<P>My personal choice?  I would go with the aluminum head one, if you don't care about the looks.  Good engines, less moving parts (no distributor to worry about), etc. etc. and decent power (I've seen 2 Fieros with these engines - both ran awesome!).<P>If you want VERY little trouble, go with the iron head engine (60 degree).  Everything switches over and you can use the red intake.<P>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Steve Normington||12-20-2001||01:53 PM||||The 3100 swap is more involved than that. The dogbone mount is on the wrong side of the engine, and you will have to use the accessories from the 3100, not the Fiero. You can't use the Fiero brackets because the bolt pattern on the side of the head is different. On the 3400 that I used, the compressor was on the wrong side of the engine, and the alternator/water pump would have required removing part of the firewall. This may not be the case with the 3100's. <P>The exhaust manifolds bolts on the aluminum head are about 1/2 inch farther apart than the bolts on the iron head, plus the ports are "D" shaped instead of round.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DKOV||12-20-2001||02:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>3.1 out of a Grand Prix is 150hp before 1994 and 165 now.<BR>3.4 is about 180HP with the Fiero intake system.<BR>The difference's in the HP is the Throttle body size. A 3.1 @ 150 has a 48mm TB the 165hp has a 52mm Tb (same size as a Fiero TB)<BR>The 3.4 has 160HP with a 48mm TB and with a 52mm gets about 180hp.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Please don't forget the DOHC or TDC 3.4L with the stock 215 HP   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>DKOV -<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Black87GT||12-20-2001||02:52 PM||||You do not really need a new 3.1 or 3.4 engine.  You can also upgrade a 2.8 engine.<P>My Fiero has a 2.8 with a 3.1 crank which makes it basically a 160 hp 3.1 engine.  I think the same trick can be done with the a 3.4 upgrade.<BR>Advantage is that you keep about the same engine weight.<P>I am not a very technical person, but for those  who are intrested I advice to look at <A HREF="http://www.engine-parts.com" TARGET=_blank>www.engine-parts.com</A>  for more detailed explanation.||||reg||4
Z||000007||dgroves||12-20-2001||09:30 PM||||The 3.1 and the 2.8 are identical with the exception of the pistons and crank. All you have to do is just rebuild the 2.8 and substitute the 3.1 crank. I have forgotten the part number for the pistons but they were stock 3.1 pistons for use with the iron head motors. The only other thing is the flywheel/flexplate. Use one from a 3.1 engine.||||reg||1
Z||000008||72vinman||12-20-2001||09:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by dgroves:<BR><B>The 3.1 and the 2.8 are identical with the exception of the pistons and crank. All you have to do is just rebuild the 2.8 and substitute the 3.1 crank. I have forgotten the part number for the pistons but they were stock 3.1 pistons for use with the iron head motors. The only other thing is the flywheel/flexplate. Use one from a 3.1 engine.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This is exactly what I have been looking to do this winter. Does anyone know the part numbers or can suggest what parts will be required for this upgrade.<BR>Thanks in advance<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigvin2_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Blacktree||12-20-2001||10:15 PM||||When it rains it pours, eh GTDude? I know the feeling. But please keep that Fiero of yours from the scrapyard. <P>I'd like to toss in a question here since we're on the subject of stroker kits and stuff. My best friend's girlfriend has a 1991 Olds Cutlass. My best bud and I were replacing the alternator in her car, which has the 3.1 liter V6 in it, and I noticed the engine had aluminum cylinder heads. <P>Immediately, the gears started turning in my head. I'm going to bore/stroke my 2.8 to 3.2 anyway, so I thought a set of aluminum heads like those would be a great addition to the engine. The big question is whether or not they'll fit on my 2.8 block. The engine <I>looks</I> like the first-gen 3.1 engine (not the 3100). Here's a shot of the engine:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.tweaktech.com/temp-pic/3point1.jpg"> <BR>So whatcha think?||||reg||1
Z||000010||yo_sweet||12-20-2001||10:24 PM||||ive got the exact motor as shown in the pic above complete from a 1990 grandprix , even have the computer harness and all im selling it for about $400 cdn there is 90,000k on it<P>very clean||||reg||1
Z||000011||Hank is Here||12-20-2001||10:55 PM||||The head will fit on the bolck.  The problem is that that is a DIS motor.  you need to use that intake to match with the ports on the head so there is no room for the distributor<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Blacktree:<BR><B>I'd like to toss in a question here since we're on the subject of stroker kits and stuff. My best friend's girlfriend has a 1991 Olds Cutlass. My best bud and I were replacing the alternator in her car, which has the 3.1 liter V6 in it, and I noticed the engine had aluminum cylinder heads. <P>Immediately, the gears started turning in my head. I'm going to bore/stroke my 2.8 to 3.2 anyway, so I thought a set of aluminum heads like those would be a great addition to the engine. The big question is whether or not they'll fit on my 2.8 block. The engine <I>looks</I> like the first-gen 3.1 engine (not the 3100). Here's a shot of the engine:<P>So whatcha think?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Blacktree||12-20-2001||11:02 PM||||Hmmm... since my 2.8 is a 1986 model, I doubt that it has accomodations for a DIS coil pack and crank trigger. Although the crankshaft I'm using came from a 1990 Lumina (and was a DIS engine). Oh well, the aluminum heads aren't worth THAT much trouble...<P>Thanks for the info, though.||||reg||1
Z||000013||00lE||12-21-2001||05:55 AM||||       <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Steve Normington:<BR><B>The 3100 swap is more involved than that. The dogbone mount is on the wrong side of the engine, and you will have to use the accessories from the 3100, not the Fiero. You can't use the Fiero brackets because the bolt pattern on the side of the head is different.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Oh..then how do you explain this??<P>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/3100_project_front_-_initial_install.jpg">       <P>Note : Uses stock Fiero 2.8 front cover, water pump, brackets, and accessories, including dogbone, alternator and A/C..  The motor is a brand new 1996 3100 with GEN III aluminum heads and 5spd Getrag transplanted into an 88 Fiero formula.<P>(pic taken shortly after install, right before initial startup, please forgive the mess)<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||1
