Z||000023||Fierobsessed||12-22-2001||12:14 PM||||It did till I got the old trusty sawzall out. Now It fits fine, the large hump had to be trimmed out flat, and the old dog bone bracket had to be cut out as well||||reg||1
Z||000024||Will||12-22-2001||02:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fierobsessed:<BR><B>It did till I got the old trusty sawzall out. Now It fits fine, the large hump had to be trimmed out flat, and the old dog bone bracket had to be cut out as well</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Adverse effects on decklid stiffness? Did you fiberglass where you had cut, or just leave it?<P>I ask because I've seen a 3800 conversion which had a similar mod. The decklid bulged up in the down position because it no longer had enough stiffness to stay straight with the tension of the springs on it.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000025||Fierobsessed||12-22-2001||07:08 PM||||my decklids old springs were both snapped when I got the car, and even if they were still good, I dont have a wing or a luggage rack so there isnt much to lift, I have not yet filled the area that I cut out for the alternator.||||reg||1
